 "deeper": [
  {
   "title": "Why Horner's syndrome gives a drooping eyelid",
   "html": "<p>Two muscles lift the upper lid. The levator palpebrae superioris is skeletal muscle on CN III and does most of the work. Behind it sits the superior tarsal muscle (Muller's muscle), smooth muscle running from the underside of the levator to the upper border of the tarsus, supplied by sympathetic fibres and worth about 1 to 3 mm of lift. Lose the sympathetic supply, as in Horner's syndrome, and you get a mild ptosis, while a CN III palsy gives a far more dramatic one.</p><p class='src'>Sources: Brainscape, Orbit and ocular adnexae, eyelids; Brainscape, Boards prep ocular anatomy.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Which gland is blocked: a stye or a chalazion",
   "html": "<p>The eyelid glands have names because they get blocked. The meibomian glands sit inside the tarsal plate and make the oil layer, so a blocked one gives a firm lump in the lid. The glands of Zeis are sebaceous glands at the base of each lash, and the glands of Moll are apocrine sweat glands next to them, so a blocked one gives a tender swelling right at the lid margin.</p><p class='src'>Sources: Duke histology, Histology of the eye and eyelid; Brainscape, Eye and eyelid.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Basal versus reflex tears",
   "html": "<p>The main lacrimal gland in the superotemporal orbit makes the reflex tears, the ones that come with onions or grief. The steady, background wetting of the eye comes largely from accessory lacrimal glands, the glands of Krause in the conjunctival fornix and the glands of Wolfring at the border of the tarsal plate. It explains why an eye can still feel dry even with a healthy main gland.</p><p class='src'>Source: Brainscape, Physiology of the eyelids.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the tear film has to be three layers",
   "html": "<p>Water alone would neither stick to the cornea nor stay there. The mucin layer from the goblet cells makes the corneal surface wettable so the water spreads instead of beading, and the oil layer from the meibomian glands caps it so it does not evaporate between blinks. Lose either outer layer and the eye is dry even if tear production is normal.</p><p class='src'>Sources: Duke histology, Histology of the eye and eyelid; lecture slides.</p>"
  }
 ],
