 "deeper": [
  {
   "title": "Why a pituitary tumour takes your peripheral vision",
   "html": "<p>The lecture's site 3 has a common clinical cause. The pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica, directly beneath the optic chiasm, so a growing pituitary adenoma pushes up into the crossing fibres from the nasal halves of both retinas. Those fibres carry the temporal (outer) halves of the visual field, so the patient loses peripheral vision on both sides: bitemporal hemianopia. Patients often notice it by bumping into door frames rather than by any change in central vision.</p><p class='src'>Sources: student flashcard sets on visual pathway lesions, cross-checked against the lecture's own site diagram.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why a blown pupil is an emergency sign",
   "html": "<p>The parasympathetic fibres from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel on the surface of the oculomotor nerve, not deep inside it. That superficial position means anything compressing the nerve from outside, such as an aneurysm or a brain herniating through the tentorium, squeezes those fibres first. So a fixed, dilated pupil can appear before any eye movement problem, which is why it is checked so urgently in head injury.</p><p class='src'>Source: Duong, Neuro-ophthalmology (review chapter on the afferent and efferent pupillary pathways).</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "The swinging torch test",
   "html": "<p>The bilateral wiring of the reflex gives a neat bedside test. Move a light quickly from one eye to the other. Normally both pupils stay constricted. If one optic nerve is damaged, shining the light into that eye sends a weaker signal, so both pupils paradoxically dilate when the light swings onto it: a relative afferent pupillary defect. It isolates a problem in the afferent limb, the optic nerve, from one in the efferent limb, CN III.</p><p class='src'>Source: student flashcard sets on the pupillary pathway, consistent with the lecture's reflex diagram.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the superior oblique needs a pulley",
   "html": "<p>The superior oblique arises at the orbital apex like the other muscles, but a muscle pulling straight back from there could only retract the eye. The trochlea solves this: the tendon hooks through it at the front of the orbit and doubles back, so the effective pull comes from in front and above. That is why a muscle arising at the back of the orbit can depress and intort the eye, and why damage to the trochlea itself can mimic a CN IV palsy.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's own description of the trochlea changing the tendon's direction, read with the muscle action grid.</p>"
  }
 ],
