 "deeper": [
  {
   "title": "Why a thyroid lump moves when you swallow",
   "html": "<p>The ligaments of Berry tie the gland to the cricoid cartilage and the first tracheal rings, and the whole larynx rides upward during swallowing. Any swelling of the thyroid therefore moves up with it. That single sign separates a thyroid mass from most other neck lumps at the bedside, and it follows directly from the lecture's relations slides.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's relation with the laryngo-tracheal axis and the ligaments of Berry.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the two recurrent nerves behave differently in surgery",
   "html": "<p>The lecture separates the right and left nerves for a reason. The right hooks around the subclavian artery high in the neck and runs a more oblique course, often in front of the inferior thyroid artery branches. The left loops under the aortic arch in the chest and ascends almost vertically in the tracheo-oesophageal groove, usually behind those branches. A surgeon looking for the nerve therefore looks in a different place on each side.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's two slides on the right and left inferior laryngeal nerves.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why hoarseness after thyroid surgery is investigated urgently",
   "html": "<p>The recurrent nerve supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid. Injure one and the cord on that side sits paramedian and does not vibrate, giving a hoarse voice. Injure both and the cords sit paramedian together, which threatens the airway rather than just the voice. That risk is why the lecture calls the relation with the recurrent nerves a major hazard of thyroidectomy.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's emphasis on recurrent nerve risk, read with the larynx lecture's account of nerve palsies.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the parathyroids are the other surgical worry",
   "html": "<p>The parathyroids are small, orange-yellow and sit in the fat right on the posteromedial border of the lobe, sometimes within the tissue the surgeon is removing. Take them all and the patient loses parathyroid hormone, calcium falls and tetany follows. The lecture pairs them with the recurrent nerves for exactly this reason: both are small, both are close, and both change the patient's life if lost.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's parathyroid gland slides and its introduction.</p>"
  }
 ],
