 "deeper": [
  {
   "title": "Why a small stroke in the internal capsule is so disabling",
   "html": "<p>The corticospinal fibres from the whole motor strip fan out over the cortex but squeeze together through the narrow internal capsule. A tiny stroke there, which in the cortex would affect only a hand or a face, can paralyse the whole opposite side at once. The lecture's capsule table is exactly why a small deep stroke causes such a large deficit.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's internal capsule description and its pyramidal tract slide.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why Parkinson's disease is a basal ganglia disease",
   "html": "<p>The lecture stresses that the basal ganglia need dopamine released by the substantia nigra. When those dopamine neurons die, the circuit that fine-tunes movement fails, giving slowness, stiffness and tremor. That is why treatment replaces dopamine, and why stimulating the subthalamic nucleus, another node in the same loop, can restore movement.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's basal ganglia, subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra slides.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why Broca and Wernicke aphasias sound so different",
   "html": "<p>Broca's area in the frontal lobe produces speech, so damage leaves the patient understanding well but speaking in slow, broken words. Wernicke's area in the temporal lobe understands language, so damage leaves fluent speech that makes little sense, and the patient cannot follow what is said. Knowing which area is where lets you locate the lesion from the way a patient speaks.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's language anatomy and lobe function slides.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why a right-sided stroke weakens the left side",
   "html": "<p>Each hemisphere controls the opposite side because the corticospinal tract crosses in the medulla. A stroke above that crossing, in the cortex or internal capsule, therefore weakens the opposite limbs. The lecture's line that a right-sided stroke weakens the left arm or leg follows directly from where the tract decussates.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's functional anatomy and pyramidal tract slides.</p>"
  }
 ],
